{"model": "Qwen/Qwen3-30B-A3B", "prompt": "In Java, wrapper classes are used to wrap primitive data types (such as int, boolean, and char) into objects. Each primitive data type has a corresponding wrapper class, such as Integer for int, Boolean for boolean, and Character for char. Wrapper classes are useful when an application needs to work with objects, but the data being manipulated is primitive.

Wrapper classes are also useful when working with certain Java collections, such as ArrayList and HashMap, which can only store objects and not primitive data types.

Here are some examples of using wrapper classes:
```
// int to Integer
int num = 10;
Integer numWrapper = new Integer(num);

// boolean to Boolean
boolean flag = true;
Boolean flagWrapper = new Boolean(flag);

// char to Character
char letter = 'A';
Character letterWrapper = new Character(letter);
```
Java also provides autoboxing and unboxing feature, which enables automatic conversion between primitive types and wrapper classes, so you don't have to create wrapper objects explicitly.
```
// Autoboxing
Integer numWrapper = 10; // int to Integer

// Unboxing
int num = numWrapper; // Integer to int
``
```", "max_tokens": 10, "ignore_eos": true, "stream": true, "stream_options": {"include_usage": true}}

